Date: Sat, 10 Apr 93 00:02:02 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Robin Hanson poses some questions:

> Two wormhole ends, one small and one big, each going very close to the
> speed of light but in opposing directions, start to form a time
> machine, even though they are very distant in space/time.  Do they 
> a) both collapse 

Seems unlikely.  Not a symmetrical situation so need for a symmetrical
solution.

> b) the small hole collapses, while the big hole flies on after
> suffering a burst of stuff through it

Yup.

> c) repel and therefore bounce off each other?

Surely the big hole is deflected less.  So it's more like the small hole
bounces off the "toughie", who is largely unaffected.

>  Unless these holes are *very* rigid, such a high speed
> "bounce" sounds more like a "smash" to me, destroying both.

I don't see why the small hole can't be smashed to smithereens and
swallowed by the bigger hole if need be.

> Only the b scenario gives your idea.

b and c, IMO

>>> [...] And with massive hole destruction, arranging meetings for
>>> negotiations might take a while in empire time.  Here's a
>>> good Schelling point problem - where should hole ends meet to merge
>>> the nets?

I take your point about the neutral zone being rather too large to
search.  Perhaps a more natural Schelling point would be the destruction
site(s).  The wreckage from their hole will now be in *your* empire time
and yours in *theirs*.  You each know the point of destruction of your
own hole, the shape of your local empire time cone and, from the
direction of the debris of the alien hole, the place of destruction of
the enemy hole can be inferred.  If each side sends a hole to each
destruction site (but more slowly, this time) communications should be
easily established.

> Hmm.. what they might want best is to invade *through* the wormholes
> and take over.

Agreed.  This is the best way to invade.  Visions of emissaries
smuggling holes in their diplomatic pouches.  :-)

> Another reason not to go tossing buoys around like
> confetti.

It's a trade off between economic growth and military security.  A
tricky problem - unless you believe, as I tend to nowadays, that
economic might brings security from invasion.  Although this assumption
might break down faced with some alien economic system - perhaps one
based around some collective, militaristic hive race...

> Robin Hanson

