Date: 9 Apr 93 02:02:00 EST
From: "Thomas D Burns" <TBURNS@gmuvax.gmu.edu>
Subject: organizations

By closely held firms I meant firms with a single owner. I think it also 
means firms that are not publicly traded, and so need not imply a 
single owner.

If just orienting on results is such a good idea, why is there only one 
company I know of that pays piece-work rates, while that used to be 
the dominant payment scheme? At the least, there are some things 
that do not lend themselves to this. There are quality problems and 
measurement problems. 

This whole discussion parallels the theory of the firm in economics. 
It started with a famous article by Ronald Coase (The Nature of the 
Firm) that asked, in essence, if markets are so great why have firms 
at all? His answer was transactions costs. Alchian and Demsetz said 
it was the difficulty of measuring marginal products for team 
production. Others (Hart? Williamson?) want to look at a firm as a 
nexus of contracts. They would say that Browne's scenario is not 
really different from what firms all do. 

I'm eclectic. I believe all the theories and more. Most of these 
theories can't explain, for instance, why a single individual might 
want to be a firm. He's not minimizing any transactions costs, 
engaging in team production, forming a contract nexus, etc.

How do you make a firm more like a spontaneous order? Some 
attempts have been made, with varying results. Internal pricing and 
internal competition are not a cure-all. 

What Dale said about rules and relationships is suggestive. It 
echoes some things I've been thinking which were inspired by 
Hayek. Hayek talks about instinctive morality, the morality of loyalty 
to the tribe, and distinguishes it from the morality of the market, the 
morality of property rights and well drawn rules. He uses them 
primarily to explain the popularity of the idea of socialism.

I want to junk the 'instinctive' part and replace it with socialization. 
As children we are surrounded by tribe-like interactions. We are 
shielded from market-type interaction for a long time. The market 
may not really have much to do (directly) with the life of a middle 
class child until s/he gets a job & moves out of the home. 

I think that people respond psychologically to this group feeling. 
Good leadership is not just about giving smart orders and incentives. 
People need to internalize the values and goals of the team they are 
a part of to be productive. Organizations have emerged to take 
advantage of this human tendency.

My explanation doesn't cover all the bases either. A company as 
large as IBM can't be a single tribe. It would need to be an 
interlocking group of such tribes. But why are such amalgams able 
to out-compete (well, for 40 years at least) markets full of small 
groups? 

