Subject: Worley's Willful Libertarian Illiteracy

Worley's deliberate libertarian-illiteracy reminds me of a talk given by
David Ramsay Steele at Laissez-Faire books recently.

Steele was a Scot socialist college student in the late 1960s and early
1970s whose main occupation was: marxist propagandist.  The college he
attended (I forget which) was unusual, he said, in that it included amongst
the Trotskyites on its faculty two libertarians.

One of them mentioned one day that Ludwig von Mises had argued in the 1920s
that it would be impossible to calculate rationally under complete socialism
like Steele advocated.  His first reaction was: "Well, that's clearly wrong."

He then set about writing a brief piece about why it was wrong, and began
researching this debate, which was carried on by Mises during the 1920s and
by Friedrich Hayek in the 1930s.

Then he changed, and began writing the piece about how socialists had to
address this argument seriously and refute it.

It evolved into a book he's just published, "From Marx to Mises," which covers
many aspects of the impossibility of socialist calculation.  In the 20-year
process of writing it, he's become a leading intellectual of the libertarian
movement, completely changing over from a socialist to a capitalist.

The talk was very funny, and perhaps his book would be interesting for a
socialist to read.

