Date: Sat, 10 Apr 93 01:19:15 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Ray Cromwell asks:
> 
>    How might one go about constructing a wormhole? I've seen proposals
> which call for 'exotic' matter to be used to prop open the throat of
> a wormhole, but is there any theoretical basis for such matter?

Exotic matter, according to some descriptions, means that the tension
(or negative pressure if you prefer), in natural units, exceeds the
energy density.  During inflation matter becomes exotic or borderline
exotic.

> Furthermore, is there evidence that we could create such matter?

No, except that if it can happen naturally, boundless optimism implies
we can create it. :-)

> (e.g. tachyons are theoretically possible, but we have no idea how to
> make them)

This is common myth in theoretical physics, and it's instructive to see
why.

Tachyons have negative mass squared - imaginary mass if you prefer.  The
mass of a particle, in quantum field theory, is associated with the
second differential of its potential as function of the field, usually
around the origin (where field = 0), where the potential's minimum is. 
A Higgs particle is a particle whose field has a hump, rather a trough,
at the origin.  Because a field will always try to adopt its minimum
energy configuration the field value usually rolls off the hill (the
'false' vacuum) into the valley.  This is called spontaneous symmetry
breaking.  The value of the field at this new minimum, somewhere in the
trough, is known as the 'true' vacuum.  Physicists then shift the origin
of their graphs, by defining new fields, to make this true vacuum value
sit at the new origin.  [this isn't just done out of spite: the
equations are easier to tie into reality if they are re-based around a
stable origin.]  By repeating this procedure for all fields with humps
(negative second differentials => imaginary masses => tachyons) at their
origins it is possible to rewrite all the equations in terms of well
behaved fields with minimums at their origins => tardons.

This way physicists have been able to forget about tachyons so
thoroughly that many believe them to be impossible (not true: they're
just highly unstable - they roll off the top of their hump very
quickly).  Tachyons are possible, but they're too evanescent to be of any
use.  We can make them, but we interpret them in terms of other tardonic
particles.  If they'll were longer lived we'd not be able to get away
with this redefinition.

Okay, so what's the morale?  Just that if something is known to be
possible it will be done (I claim).  Tachyons are not a counter-proof
to this dictum (the Feinberg Principle - anything not expressly
forbidden is doable, eventually).

>  Can someone cite some references to speculations about how to 
>  make these [wormholes/exotic matter] things?

I've already quoted all the references I know on the subject.  Sorry.

>  Also, how do you move them? Apply an electric field?

Once built, :-), move the exotic matter and the wormhole follows.  Apply
a shear across the mouths and they separate.
> 
> -- Ray Cromwell

