>>freer than the other? 
>
>Given the specific comparison in question (a society that lets me accept a
>higher salary for providing proof of bathing to my employer versus a
>society that does not) I thought the criterion is obvious:  which allows a
>wider range of behaviors?  I did not intend to go swooping off into

Each has freedoms the other lacks. The question is which freedoms do we 
prefer to do without?

>philosophical abstractions.  But none the less, here we go . . . 8-(
>
If you'd rather not, then don't. X^}

>>The implicit criterion is absolute ability
>>to write and enforce any contract.  I don't like this criterion
>>because it enables enforceable murder contracts and slavery
>>contracts. I'm not suggesting that is what Tom definitely
>>has in mind, but the principle seem the same to me.
>
>>Dave Burns
>
>The principles are not necessarily the same.  To make the leap from my
>example (freedom to accept higher pay in return for proof of bathing) to
>one of your examples (murder) is to ignore that fact that my employment
>contract violates no other person's property rights.  Murder, however,
>violates others' property in their persons.

This simply assumes what is to be shown. Property rights are not 
platonic ideals, clear, distinct, and pre-supplied. The question is what the
structure and content of property rights should be; what is ownable, tradeable
enforceable. The society that limits enforcement of employment contracts
views this as a 'property in their persons' of employees. The other does not.

>
>Slavery is a trickier issue; suffice it to say that reasonable libertarians
>disagree about whether or not slavery contracts should be enforced.  You
>may not "like" slavery contracts, but you should recognize that there are
>cogent arguments on their behalf.  

Slavery contracts differ because (possibly) no non-consenting 3rd 
party is involved, just 2 parties to the contract. Is that the cogent argument?
My whole point is to question whether that is sufficient and whether Tom 
believes that it is.

>
>(I'll note, by the way, that some avowed libertarians would support the
>case for enforcing murder contracts.  Anyone who allows private defense or
>protection agencies had better consider the issue!  I'll stay agnostic for
>now, though, and simply argue that I don't think many people would agree to
>join a PPL that enforced murder contracts amongst its members.)
>

By similar reasoning, PPLs that enforce slavery contracts
or refuse to hear any arguments about unconscionable contracts
may go looking for customers.

The weak point of Tom's case is that he takes a specific view of
property rights for granted. Property rights vary considerably from place
to place and I doubt that adoption of PPL will exert a powerful unifying
force on them. In fact, perhaps quite the opposite will occur.

The weak point of my case is that when courts interfere in contracts
they circumvent an important feedback mechanism for keeping the law
robust. If it becomes difficult to contract around a precedent or ruling,
the law rigidifies and the judge becomes a usurper. 
If the PPL is competitive (there are many courts that could hear the
case) and no court will endorse slavery contracts (or certain employment
practices) should it then be called unjust?
