Waaaaaaiiiiit a minute!  This whole thing started when you asked me to
define my use of "freedom"!  So now I'll turn the tables: What criterion
are *you* using?  Not (so far as I can see) one that compares allowable
ranges of behaviors.

>The society that limits enforcement of employment contracts
>views this as a 'property in their persons' of employees. The other does not.

You've lost me here.  I have never heard anyone defend limits on employment
contracts by saying potential employees have property rights of some sort. 
The arguments instead typically involve policy issues, equitable pleas, and
paens to privacy.

>Slavery contracts differ because (possibly) no non-consenting 3rd 
>party is involved, just 2 parties to the contract. Is that the cogent argument?
>My whole point is to question whether that is sufficient and whether Tom 
>believes that it is.

That is a cogent argument, and the main one.  I don't think it's
sufficient, given that there are powerful counterarguments to enforcing
slavery contracts.  It's a difficult and complex debate, with myriads of
argument, counterarguments, factual claims, and moral pleas.

>. . . PPLs that enforce slavery contracts
>or refuse to hear any arguments about unconscionable contracts
>may go looking for customers.

Who can argue with "may"?  It's always good for a little mileage, I
suppose.  But because slavery contracts do not put third parties in harm's
way, they are less likely to be selected against than murder contracts are.
 Many people have in times past chosen to enter into indentured servitude
agreements, and people may in times future similarly agree on slavery
contracts.  Cynics would claim that many people already do!

>The weak point of Tom's case is that he takes a specific view of
>property rights for granted. 

I take them for granted in this sense: I accept as a working assumption
that people typically act to defend themselves from harm.  That's pretty
uncontroversial, and all I need to distinguish the murder from the slavery
hypos.

>Property rights vary considerably from place
>to place and I doubt that adoption of PPL will exert a powerful unifying
>force on them. In fact, perhaps quite the opposite will occur.

I think this depends on what sort of property rights you are talking about.
 Rights to person?  No, they are pretty universal and I would expect PPLs
to protect them very consistently.  Intellectual property?  Yeah, that
varies a lot and I would expect PPLs to offer many different protection
schemes.

