Communications Daily

April 1, 1993, Thursday

SECTION: Vol. 13, No. 62; Pg. 1

LENGTH: 815 words

HEADLINE: Spectrum Auctions Pushed; MARKEY CALLS NATIONAL
'INFOSTRUCTURE' KEY TO MULTIMEDIA FUTURE

 BODY:

Govt. encouragement of national telecommunications "infostructure" is
key to aiding transition to multimedia world, House Telecom
Subcommittee Chmn. Markey (D-Mass.) told multimedia conference in
Washington Wed. sponsored by Center for Strategic & International
Studies. He pressed for spectrum auctions, elimination of lotteries
and use of digital compression over twisted telephone pairs as interim
steps toward nationwide broadband network, strengthening pioneer's
preferences.

Congress should rescind FCC authority to use spectrum lotteries, said
Markey, who plans hearing April 15 on auctions. He also suggested that
small entrepreneurs be allowed to pay royalties rather than auction
fees to govt. for use of spectrum to "ensure that these big ideas with
small pockets can get to the consumers." Specifically on issue of
multimedia, Markey said, "content, not the conduit, will be the
arbiter of ultimate success" and, referring to "killer" computer
applications such as Lotus 1-2-3 that drove personal computer market,
"finding the killer application [for multimedia] may prove elusive in
the short run." Govt. role in encouraging multimedia should be
creating viable platforms, not judging technologies, Markey said, and
first priority is upgrading telecommunications network to carry
multimedia.

Current hype on multimedia is "a bit scary" because it could backfire
on industry, Bell Labs Technology Dir. Gregory Blonder said. He
predicted that such hyped uses as distance medicine and transmitting
Library of Congress materials aren't "panacea." Peter Fannon, exec.
dir., Advanced TV Test Center, agreed that multimedia is "just lights
and wires in a box unless the content is meaningful." Viacom Senior Vp
Edward Horowitz said consumer isn't interested in technology, just in
content, and doesn't want to pay for higher quality than he or she
needs.

Most of world doesn't care about multimedia, CNN International Sales
Managing Dir. Mark Rudolph said. Noting that 50% of world population
doesn't have access to basic TV now, he predicted that "maybe" 1% of
world will have access to multimedia by 2000. Knight-Ridder Design Lab
Dir. Roger Fidler said multimedia combined with "personal digital
appliances" will provide new delivery method for newspapers: "We must
decouple the words news and paper." In response to question whether
advertising would be viable in fragmented multimedia world, Fidler
said multimedia "can create an environment that is much more exciting
for advertisers." Hugh Williams of BBC World Service said consumers
would be willing to pay for multimedia: "If you create a demand, you
can create a price."

Time Warner Telecommunications CEO Dennis Patrick, former FCC chmn.,
said company's planned Orlando "full-service network" (CD Jan 27 p1)
is step toward multimedia because it will be convergence of cable,
interactivity, video-on-demand, telecommunications. He said
full-service network is way to move gradually toward full multimedia
because each stage of development will generate own revenue sources
and individually can be justified economically: "The very high-end
applications are businesses that people will lose a lot of money on.
You have to build a network on businesses that you know a lot about
and know you can pay for with incremental business." Orlando project
eventually will make word cable obsolete, Patrick predicted, because
result will be switched broadband network that can provide all kinds
of telecommunications services.

Other comments at conference: (1) CableLabs Technology Dir. Aleksander
Futro pushed role of cable in multimedia, saying it provides broadband
capability to 97% of population and full upgrade would cost $20
billion, vs. $400 billion for national telecommunications
infrastructure. (2) National Semiconductor Vp John McElroy said his
company will have multimedia LANs operating next year, but wide area
networks are stalled because of lack of network capacity. (3) Viacom's
Horowitz said Asymmetrical Digital Subscriber Line ( ADSL) isn't good
enough because it's only VCR-quality. (4) Compagnie G n rale des Eaux
Exec. Vp Michel Villaneau said cable operators are trying to design
cable systems so that they're as compatible as possible with future
developments such as multimedia. (5) France Telecom Vp Alain Giraud
joked that multimedia revenue has "grown very fast from zero million
dollars last year to zero billion dollars this year." (6) GTE Spacenet
Mktg. Dir. John Schmuhl said it's too easy to focus on multimedia
delivery systems: "The components are not new. The dream is to put
together in a package something that is more than the sum of its
parts." (7) Lunch speaker MPAA Pres. Jack Valenti pushed for more open
markets for U.S. program producers, saying that "all the U.S. industry
wants is to be treated in the same way that Europeans find so
seductive in our market."

